        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is asking a technical question about file redirection in their test scripts. They want to know:\n\n1. When they run tests, they redirect output to `build/main_out.txt` and `build/error.txt`\n2. Do these files get overwritten on each test run?\n3. What happens to the old files - are they deleted or overwritten?\n\nThis is purely a technical question about Windows/PowerShell file redirection behavior. I should answer based on facts about how the shell handles output redirection (`>` operator).\n\nIn PowerShell and cmd.exe, the `>` redirection operator **overwrites** the file (creates it or truncates it). The `>>` operator appends. So yes, each run replaces the previous content. The old content is gone — the file is truncated and rewritten, not deleted separately (the old data is simply overwritten at the filesystem level).\n\nLet me answer concisely and in Russian, as per AGENTS.md language rule.",
