          "text": "The user is asking me to explain again why order-only prerequisites prevent false triggers. Let me think about this carefully.\n\nThe scenario:\n\n1. `build/t1_dist_matrix_c/main.obj: t1_dist_matrix_c/main.c | build/t1_dist_matrix_c`\n\nIf `build/t1_dist_matrix_c` were a **normal** prerequisite (before `|`), then every time make evaluates this rule, it checks if the directory is \"newer\" than the target. The directory's modification time changes whenever files inside it are created or modified. So after compiling `main.obj` into that directory, the directory's timestamp gets updated. Next time make runs, it sees the directory is newer than `main.obj` — and recompiles unnecessarily.\n\nBut the timestamp change happens because `main.obj` was placed there BY make itself during the previous build. So it's a self-inflicted recompilation cycle.\n\nWith **order-only** (after `|`), make only checks if the prerequisite **exists** (for the purpose of ordering), but does NOT use its timestamp to decide whether the target needs rebuilding. So the directory's timestamp doesn't matter at all for triggering recompilation.\n\nThe user also asked for a simpler name analog. Let me think of a good one.\n\nAnalogy for prerequisites vs order-only: think of building a piece of furniture.\n\nNormal prerequisites = \"the wood and the instructions\". If either changes (new wood, updated instructions), you redo the work.\n\nOrder-only prerequisite = \"the workshop table\". You need it to exist before you start, but whether the table was built 1 hour ago or 1 year ago doesn't affect whether the furniture needs rework. You just need it to be there.",
