mmend order-only for directories PRECISELY because:\n- A directory's timestamp changes whenever a file inside is added, renamed, or deleted\n- If your build creates `main.obj` in that directory, the directory timestamp gets set to now\n- Next time make runs: `main.obj` is OLD (from previous build), directory is NEW (just had something written to it)\n- So make sees prerequisite (directory) newer than target (`main.obj`) → rebuild\n\nThis CAN happen even on Windows, especially if the clock granularity is coarse or if there's any delay between file creation and the check.\n\nThe user is questioning: shouldn't the directory time be the same as main.obj time?\n\nAnswer: Not necessarily. The directory's mtime updates when the DIRECTORY's metadata changes (adding/removing an entry). The `main.obj` file's mtime updates when the FILE's content changes. They're two different timestamps. When `main.obj` is created:\n1. File's mtime = time of write\n2. Directory's mtime = time of directory entry modification\n\nThese are two different operations and may not have exactly the same timestamp, especially on NTFS where granularity may vary.\n\nBut more importantly: on a subsequent build where source hasn't changed, `main.obj` still has its old timestamp from the previous build. If something else was written to the directory (e.g., a `*.d` dependency file, or a temp file from a previous build step), the directory will have a newer timestamp than `main.obj`. Even if nothing else was written, directory and file mtime are separate and can drift.\n\nThe safe bet, accepted universally in make practice, is order-only for directories. */usr/share/doc/make-doc/ or any GNU make tutorial will say the same.*",
