        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "So the current .editorconfig doesn't specify charset at all. And there's no .gitattributes file.\n\nThe user's current approach (no charset in .editorconfig) actually works fine in practice because:\n1. FAR Editor saves in CP1251 by default on Russian Windows\n2. The bat files set `chcp 1251` at runtime\n3. The C/C++ code uses `setlocale(LC_ALL, \"Russian_Russia.1251\")` explicitly\n\nThe question is whether adding anything to .editorconfig would help or confuse.\n\nMy take: .editorconfig simply cannot represent CP1251. The `charset` values supported are: `latin1`, `utf-8`, `utf-8-bom`, `utf-16be`, `utf-16le`. None of these match CP1251. Setting `latin1` would be misleading since it's a different encoding in the upper half.\n\nThe practical solutions:\n1. **Do nothing in .editorconfig** — leave charset unspecified (current state), rely on the fact that editors respect the file's actual bytes and the project convention\n2. **`.gitattributes`** — can tell git how to handle encoding for display/diff purposes\n3. **Document in README** — but the user said not to create docs unless asked\n\nI think the honest answer is: .editorconfig cannot express CP1251, and trying to set `latin1` would be actively misleading. The current approach (omitting charset) is the best option. For telling editors about the encoding, different editors need different approaches (FAR: set in editor settings; VS Code: via `files.encoding` in settings.json, but it can also read from `.editorconfig`).\n\nActually, I should clarify: VS Code and some other editors DO try to use `.editorconfig` charset value. If you set `charset = latin1`, VS Code would try to open the file as Latin-1, which would render Russian text incorrectly (since CP1251 and Latin-1 differ in the 128-255 range). So setting `latin1` would be actively harmful.\n\nLet me give a straightforward answer.",
