          "text": "The user is asking why .editorconfig doesn't support ANSI/CP1251. This is a general knowledge question, not about editing files. I can answer this.\n\n\"ANSI\" here refers to Windows code pages like CP1251 (for Russian). The .editorconfig specification was designed primarily around UTF-8 as the universal standard, and the charset values they chose to support are the most common cross-platform encodings.\n\nLet me think about the actual technical/political reasons:\n1. `.editorconfig` was created to solve cross-editor formatting consistency (spaces/tabs, indent size, end-of-line). Charset was added later as a secondary concern.\n2. The charset values they support are limited to: `latin1`, `utf-8`, `utf-8-bom`, `utf-16be`, `utf-16le`. These cover the most common encodings in cross-platform development.\n3. Windows-specific single-byte code pages (CP1251, CP1252, etc.) are platform-specific and would add complexity. Supporting all Windows code pages would mean supporting dozens of values.\n4. The practical reality: UTF-8 has become the universal standard, and most modern development happens in UTF-8. CP1251/ANSI is Windows-legacy-specific.\n\nI should keep this answer short and factual.",
